.TH SIMPLE 1 "2024-01-01" "simple 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
simple \- a simple test program that prints messages
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B simple
[\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR]
[\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR]
[\fB\-c\fR|\fB\-\-count\fR \fINUMBER\fR]
[\fIFILE\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B simple
program demonstrates basic command line functionality by printing messages to stdout.
It can read from a file or stdin and repeat the output a specified number of times.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display help information and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Enable verbose output mode.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-count " \fINUMBER\fR"
Repeat output NUMBER times (default: 1).
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I FILE
Input file to read. If not specified, reads from stdin.
.SH EXAMPLES
Print "Hello, World!" once:
.EX
$ simple
Hello, World!
.EE

Print "Hello, World!" three times:
.EX
$ simple --count 3
Hello, World!
Hello, World!
Hello, World!
.EE

Read from a file with verbose output:
.EX
$ echo "Test message" > input.txt
$ simple --verbose input.txt
Reading from: input.txt
Test message
.EE

Show help:
.EX
$ simple --help
Usage: simple [OPTIONS] [FILE]
.EE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
General error (invalid arguments, file not found, etc.).
.TP
.B 2
Usage error (invalid command line syntax).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /dev/stdin
Default input when no FILE is specified.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cat (1),
.BR echo (1)
